  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 935 PM MDT Tue Jun 30 2026\n\nThere is little change in the longwave pattern through the end \nof the week, as troughing dominates the west and ridging to the \neast. This keeps eastern Utah and western Colorado under breezy \nsouthwesterly flow. Afternoon gusts of 20-30 mph are expected \ndaily. Passing shortwaves will bring periods of higher wind \ngusts, 25-35 mph, with locally up to 40 mph. Additionally, these\nwaves will have just enough moisture with them to create a few \nvirga showers or dry thunderstorms over northeastern Utah, \nraising the concern for lightning starts. Surface levels remain \nextremely dry, with daytime minimum humidity values in the \nsingle digits for the lower elevations, and the low teens higher\nup. As a result, Critical Fire Weather conditions remain with \nus through at least the end of the week. Red Flag Warnings are \nin effect from noon to 9PM Wednesday, with Fire Weather Watches \nin effect from noon to 9PM Thursday. \n\nThe slightly cooler airmass associated with the trough will \nkeep our temperatures at or a few degrees below normal through \nthe end of the week. However, by the weekend flow aloft begins \nto shift zonal as high pressure tries to build into the \nsouthwest. Winds will finally relax, and temperatures will jump \nto around 5 degrees above normal in response. There are hints \nthat some subtropical moisture will work its way northward under\nthe high by early next week, however the overall pattern isn't \nthat favorable for a good tap of deep moisture. With the high \ndirectly over the Desert Southwest, moisture is more likely to \nreach areas to our east and west. And with dry surface \nconditions still in place, virga showers, gusty winds, and dry \nthunderstorms are more likely that any substantial wetting rain.",
